  "query_preview": "..\nIssued 250 PM CDT Tue Sep 15 2026\n\nThursday through Tuesday:\n\nWhile the surface frontal boundary will be south of the WI/IL\nborder Thursday morning, the broad, weak 850mb front will \ncontinue to sit over northern IL. While the overall forcing will\nbe too weak for an organized area of rain, we cannot rule out a\nfew light showers or sprinkles through the whole day. In \naddition, there could be a push of cooler air down the axis of \nLake Michigan that converges over southeast WI and could produce a\nfew showers along it in the late afternoon and evening.\n\nWarm air advection will resume Thursday night over eastern Iowa.\nThis is associated with a mid level shortwave trough \napproaching from the northern Plains. The 850mb warm front (and \nrain) will slowly spread into southwest WI Friday morning. \nHowever, the system will be occluding which will slow down the \nprogression of this front into southern WI. \n\nThere is highest confidence in widespread showers and a few\nthunderstorms impacting southern WI from late Friday evening \nthrough Saturday morning. This is when the mid level shortwave \ntrough and surface low track across southern WI. We will have the\nfront focused right overhead for about a twelve hour period with \nthe low level jet veering right over us, precipitable water values\nwill be around 2 inches again, and we will be along the\ninstability gradient. These ingredients put us in a favorable\nposition for a heavy rain risk. Keep up with the forecast with\nthis scenario. \n\nSunday is looking drier for us, although lingering clouds and\nlight rain/sprinkles should still be possible. Then yet another\nmid level shortwave trough trekking across the Northern Plains\nwill arrive in the Upper Midwest by Sunday night or Monday morning\nand bring more rain chances to southern WI. \n\nCronce",
